# Creating a project

```sh
howdy new my_app
```

`howdy new` creates a Howdy project in a new folder. The project is a Gleam
application with:

- a themed home page built from [Howdy UI](/docs/ui/pages), rendered
  through a layout in `src/<project>/layouts/`
- static assets served from `priv/public`
- hot reload for development, with the [admin](/docs/admin/overview) at
  `/_howdy` and a [console](/docs/guides/console) ready to attach
- a test suite, and `howdy check` to run it
- a `Dockerfile` and a health check at `/up`, ready to
  [deploy](/docs/guides/deployment)
- `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/skills/`, guides for coding agents
  working on the project

By default it also has a SQLite database with an example todo list: a
migration, a `todos` feature with its record, database operations and a JSON
API at `/api/todos`, and a live todo list on the home page, rendered as a
[live component](/docs/ui/live-components).

## Project names

The name must be a valid Gleam project name: lowercase letters, digits and
underscores, starting with a letter. It cannot be the name of a module that
ships with Erlang, such as `pg`, `auth` or `timer`, which the project's own
module would replace.

You can give a path instead, and the last segment becomes the project name:

```sh
howdy new apps/my_app
```

## Choosing a database

`--database` picks the database, and `sqlite` is the default. SQLite needs no
setup: the database is a file, created and migrated the first time the app
starts.

```sh
howdy new my_app --database postgres
```

`--database postgres` builds the same project on PostgreSQL. The app
connects through a connection pool configured from `DATABASE_URL`, and the
project gets a `docker-compose.yml` that starts PostgreSQL with a development
database and a test database.

```sh
howdy new my_app --no-database
```

`--no-database` leaves out the database and the todo list, which gives you a
page-only project. The [`howdy create`](/docs/cli/create) commands that
write a table, and the [`howdy add`](/docs/cli/add) packages that keep
their data in the database, need one, so they do not work in a project made
this way.

`--database` and `--no-database` cannot be used together.

## Leaving things out

```sh
howdy new my_app --minimal
```

`--minimal` leaves out the dev admin, telemetry and the console, for a
project that wants nothing beyond the server.

```sh
howdy new my_api --api-only
```

`--api-only` makes a JSON backend with no pages. It leaves out Howdy UI,
Lustre, the home page, the layouts, the static files and the HTML 404 page.
With a database it keeps the todos JSON API. Packages added later follow suit: `howdy add auth` in
such a project adds the JSON API without the sign-in pages.

```sh
howdy new my_app --no-agents
```

`--no-agents` leaves out `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/skills/`.

```sh
howdy new my_app --no-docker
```

`--no-docker` leaves out the `Dockerfile`, `.dockerignore` and `bin/start`,
for an app deployed some other way. The rest of
[Ready to deploy](#Ready-to-deploy) still applies, and
[`howdy add docker`](/docs/cli/add) adds the files later.

## Ready to deploy

Every project runs in production the way it runs in development, and a
platform can manage it:

- `GET /up` answers `{"status":"ok"}` while the app runs, for load
  balancers and container platforms. It is a
  [health check](/docs/guides/health-checks), answered before the routes
  and middleware and never logged.
- `main` starts the server as an OTP application, so on `SIGTERM` it stops
  taking connections and gives requests in flight 8 seconds to finish. See
  [Supervision and shutdown](/docs/guides/supervision).
- With a database, the app applies pending migrations as it starts, holding
  a lock so copies that start together take turns. `MIGRATE_ON_START=false`
  leaves them to a step of their own.
- `HOST` and `PORT` say where it listens; it reads its configuration from
  the environment.

It also gets three files for containers:

- `Dockerfile` builds an image of the app with
  `gleam export erlang-shipment`, on the Gleam and Erlang/OTP versions
  installed where you ran `howdy new`, and runs it as an unprivileged user
  with a `HEALTHCHECK` on `/up`.
- `.dockerignore` keeps `build/`, `.env` and local databases out of it.
- `bin/start` starts the app in the image: `run` serves it, `migrate`
  applies the migrations and exits, and `remote` opens a shell inside the
  running app.

With PostgreSQL, `docker-compose.yml` can also run the image beside the
database, with `docker compose --profile app up --build`.
[Deploying](/docs/guides/deployment) takes it from there.

## What happens

`howdy new` works through these steps, and shows its progress as it goes:

1. It checks that `gleam` is installed, and warns you if `erl` is missing.
2. It writes the project files.
3. It formats them with `gleam format`.
4. It downloads the dependencies with `gleam deps download`.
5. It prints what to do next.

Downloading the dependencies needs a network connection and Git. Pass
`--skip-deps` to leave that step out, and run `gleam deps download` yourself
later:

```sh
howdy new my_app --skip-deps
```

To see the files a command would write without writing them:

```sh
howdy new my_app --dry-run --json
```

## Which Howdy release

Every Howdy package in a new project comes from the same release tag, such
as `ref = "v1.0.0"`, so they always match and never change under you. By
default that is the release the CLI's templates were written for. Pass
`--howdy` to choose another:

```sh
howdy new my_app --howdy 1.0.0
```

A release the templates predate may need changes to the generated code.
The project's README says which release it uses and how to upgrade it.

## Flags

- `--database sqlite` or `--database postgres`: the database to use.
  Defaults to `sqlite`.
- `--no-database`: leave out the database and the todo list example.
- `--minimal`: leave out the admin, telemetry and the console.
- `--api-only`: a JSON backend only, without Howdy UI, pages, layouts or
  static files.
- `--no-agents`: leave out `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/skills/`.
- `--no-docker`: leave out the `Dockerfile`, `.dockerignore` and
  `bin/start`.
- `--howdy 1.0.0`: the Howdy release to depend on. Defaults to the one the
  CLI was written for.
- `--skip-deps`: do not run `gleam deps download` after creating the files.
- `--dry-run`: show which files would be created, without writing them.
- `--json`: print the result as one JSON object.
- `--plain`: print progress as plain lines instead of animating.

## Next

Move into the project and start it with `howdy dev`. If you chose
PostgreSQL, start the database first with `docker compose up -d`.
[Getting started](/docs/guides/getting-started) walks through the first run.
